\begin{note}
Sed scripts are just ordinary shell words,
and so all of the quoting rules from Topic 01 apply to them.
This creates a notorious source of bugs because the characters
\lstinline{$} and \lstinline{^} mean different things to the shell and to sed.

To sed, \lstinline{^} matches the beginning of a line and \lstinline{$} matches the end of a line.
To the shell, \lstinline{$} is the variable expansion operator.
The shell expands variables inside double quotes \lstinline{"} but not inside single quotes \lstinline{'}.
Therefore:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\lstinline{sed 's/hello$/x/'} anchors the match to the end of the line,
because the shell passes \lstinline{$} through untouched.
\item
\lstinline{sed "s/hello$/x/"} also anchors to the end of the line,
but only because \lstinline{$/} is not a valid variable name so the shell leaves the \lstinline{$} alone.
This is a dangerous accident, not a guarantee.
\item
\lstinline{sed "s/$foo/x/"} substitutes the value of the shell variable \lstinline{foo} into the regex.
\item
\lstinline{sed 's/$foo/x/'} matches the four literal characters \lstinline{$foo} is \emph{not} what happens;
in a POSIX basic regular expression, \lstinline{$} is only an anchor at the very end of the regex,
and so it is treated as the literal character \lstinline{$} followed by \lstinline{foo}.
\end{enumerate}
The \lstinline{^} character is never special to the shell, so it behaves identically inside both quote types.
\end{note}
